"<div> <span>On the Detection of Local and Global Amoebas: Theoretical Insights and Practical Algorithms</span> </div> by René González-Martínez, marcos laffitte, Amanda Montejano :: SSRN (https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/Delivery.cfm/f0c80726-6c3c-440d-8b94-825230782cdd-MECA.pdf?abstractid=5459765&mirid=1)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; On the Detection of Local and Global Amoebas: Theoretical Insights and Practical Algorithms ... Our work includes a proof that almost every graph is not an amoeba, and the identi cation of a special type of edge-replacement called weird-edge-replacements. ... On the Detection of Local and Global Amoebas: Theoretical Insights and Practical Algorithms\n\nDownload This Paper\n\nOpen PDF in Browser\n\n\n# \n\nOn the Detection of Local and Global Amoebas: Theoretical Insights and Practical Algorithms\n\n39 Pages Posted: 8 Sep 2025\n\nSee all articles by René González-Martínez\n\n## René González-Martínez\n\naffiliation not provided to SSRN\n\n## marcos laffitte\n\naffiliation not provided to SSRN\n\n## Amanda Montejano\n\naffiliation not provided to SSRN\n\n### Abstract\n\nWestudy local and global amoebas, which are graphs that have unique properties with respect to interpolation techniques in graphs. Our work includes a proof that almost every graph is not an amoeba, and the identi cation of a special type of edge-replacement called weird-edge-replacements. Additionally, we provide an innite family of trees that are both weird local and global amoebas. Our contributions extend to the development and implementation of several algorithms for detecting local and global amoebas, which are made available in a public repository along with multiple examples.\n\nKeywords: Amoeba edge-replacement graph-isomorphisms, interpolation\n\nLicense Information\n\nThe copyright holder has granted SSRN a license. All rights reserved. No reuse allowed without permission.\n\nSuggested Citation\n\nGonzález-Ma